--- !u!114 &807114088
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 807114087}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 2e6ef2ee37c29f54980a8069743fdcc2, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  availableModels:
  - buttonID: General
    modelAsset: {fileID: 5022602860645237092, guid: 5e169beabcd38f743a2ff50d7efcf50d, type: 3}
    diseases:
    - diseaseName: ALS
      solution: Apply copper-based fungicides and practice crop rotation.
    - diseaseName: Angular Leafspot
      solution: Use copper-based treatments, avoid overhead watering (irrigate at
        the base), and destroy infected plant debris.
    - diseaseName: Anthracnose Fruit Rot
      solution: Remove and destroy infected fruits. Apply preventive fungicides (e.g.,
        copper-based or chlorothalonil).
    - diseaseName: Bean Rust
      solution: Plant resistant varieties, ensure good air circulation, and apply
        rust-specific fungicides at the first sign of infection.
    - diseaseName: Blossom Blight
      solution: Prune and burn affected branches/flowers. Apply preventive fungicide
        treatments during the bud and blooming stages.
    - diseaseName: Gray Mold
      solution: Improve ventilation, reduce humidity (especially in greenhouses),
        and use Botrytis-specific fungicides.
    - diseaseName: Leaf Spot
      solution: Remove diseased leaves, ensure proper spacing between plants, and
        apply broad-spectrum fungicides.
    - diseaseName: Powdery Mildew Fruit
      solution: Apply sulfur-based treatments, neem oil, or potassium bicarbonate
        to protect fruits from mildew.
    - diseaseName: Powdery Mildew Leaf
      solution: Spray leaves with mildew-specific fungicides (sulfur-based). Avoid
        excessive nitrogen fertilization.
    - diseaseName: Disease
      solution: 'Generic identification: Requires a more detailed visual inspection
        to determine the correct treatment. Maintain good plant hygiene.'
    - diseaseName: leaf mold
      solution: Decrease air humidity, increase ventilation, and apply preventive
        fungicides (common in greenhouse tomatoes).
    - diseaseName: spider mites
      solution: This is a pest, not a disease. Treat with neem oil, insecticidal
        soap, or specific miticides. Regular water misting can deter their spread.
    - diseaseName: Undetected
      solution: Undetected, try again!
    confidenceThreshold: 0.25
  - buttonID: Castraveti
    modelAsset: {fileID: 5022602860645237092, guid: 5e169beabcd38f743a2ff50d7efcf50d, type: 3}
    diseases:
    - diseaseName: anthracnose
      solution: Apply copper-based fungicides and practice crop rotation.
    - diseaseName: angular_lesions
      solution: Use copper-based treatments, avoid overhead watering (irrigate at
        the base), and destroy infected plant debris.
    - diseaseName: healthy
      solution: Remove and destroy infected fruits. Apply preventive fungicides (e.g.,
        copper-based or chlorothalonil).
    - diseaseName: leaf_miner
      solution: Plant resistant varieties, ensure good air circulation, and apply
        rust-specific fungicides at the first sign of infection.
    confidenceThreshold: 0.25
  - buttonID: apple
    modelAsset: {fileID: 5022602860645237092, guid: d8ddc6e6080568e4391555a582e2751a, type: 3}
    diseases:
    - diseaseName: apple scab
      solution: 'Prevention: The fungus overwinters in fallen leaves. Rake and destroy
        them, or apply a 5% urea spray to the fallen leaves in autumn to speed up
        their decomposition.


        Action: Prune the tree canopy during the dormant
        season to ensure good air circulation and sunlight penetration, which helps
        leaves dry faster after rain.


        Treatment: Apply a contact fungicide
        (like Captan) or a systemic one (like Myclobutanil) starting at the green
        tip stage and continuing every 10-14 days through the spring rainy season.'
    - diseaseName: black rot
      solution: 'Prevention: Remove any "mummies" (dried, shriveled, infected fruits)
        left hanging on the tree during winter, as they are the primary source of
        spring spores.


        Action: Cut out all dead, diseased, or cankered
        branches. Always make cuts at least 15 cm below the visible damage into healthy
        wood. Burn the pruned wood.


        Treatment: Apply a Copper-based fungicide
        or Captan immediately after pruning and during the early fruit development
        stages.'
    - diseaseName: cedar apple rust
      solution: 'Prevention: This fungus requires two hosts. If possible, remove
        all Juniper (Cedar) trees within a 2-mile radius of the apple orchard.


        Action:
        Inspect nearby junipers in early spring for orange, gelatinous galls and
        prune them out before they release spores.


        Treatment: Apple trees
        must be protected with fungicides (like Myclobutanil) starting at the pink
        bud stage and continuing until 3 weeks after petal fall.'
    - diseaseName: healthy
      solution: 'Status: The tree is vigorous and shows no signs of fungal or bacterial
        distress.


        Maintenance: Continue annual structural pruning. Apply
        a dormant horticultural oil spray in late winter to suffocate overwintering
        insect eggs (like aphids and mites).'
    confidenceThreshold: 0.25
  - buttonID: corn
    modelAsset: {fileID: 5022602860645237092, guid: 18adc99812e83b74584e324f44092ae6, type: 3}
    diseases:
    - diseaseName: cercospora leaf spot gray leaf spot
      solution: 'Prevention: Practice at least a one-to-two-year crop rotation away
        from corn. Use deep tillage to completely bury infected crop debris where
        the fungus survives.


        Action: Choose corn hybrids with high genetic
        resistance to Gray Leaf Spot for future plantings.


        Treatment: If
        lesions reach the ear leaf before the silking stage, aerial application of
        a fungicide (like Pyraclostrobin or Azoxystrobin) is economically justified
        to save the yield.'
    - diseaseName: common rust
      solution: 'Prevention: Ensure plants are not seeded at an excessively high
        density, allowing wind to circulate and dry the leaves.


        Action:
        Monitor the field early in the season. Rust thrives in cool, humid weather
        and usually slows down as summer heat arrives.


        Treatment: Chemical
        treatment is rarely needed for commercial grain corn, but for sweet corn
        or severe early infections, apply a targeted fungicide as soon as the reddish
        pustules spread rapidly.'
    - diseaseName: healthy
      solution: 'Status: The crop is clean and developing robustly.


        Maintenance:
        Keep the field free of competitive weeds. Ensure an adequate water supply,
        especially during the critical tasseling and grain-filling (blister) periods.'
    - diseaseName: northern leaf blight
      solution: 'Prevention: Select highly resistant corn hybrids. Destroy or deeply
        plow crop residues immediately after harvest.


        Action: Monitor lower
        leaves for large, "cigar-shaped" grayish-green lesions.


        Treatment:
        Apply fungicide treatments only if the disease is spreading rapidly to the
        upper canopy before the tasseling phase. Late-season infections usually do
        not impact yield enough to warrant spraying.'
    confidenceThreshold: 0.25
  - buttonID: cassava
    modelAsset: {fileID: 5022602860645237092, guid: d9143cd8339534b4d92c1e9c92ca51d1, type: 3}
    diseases:
    - diseaseName: Bacterial Blight (CBB)
      solution: 'Prevention: Plant only certified, disease-free stem cuttings. Rotate
        cassava with non-host crops (like maize or legumes) for at least one year.


        Action:
        Avoid moving through the field when plants are wet (from dew or rain), as
        bacteria spread easily through water droplets. Uproot and burn heavily infected
        plants immediately.


        Treatment: Chemical control is generally ineffective
        once the plant is severely infected, but copper-based bactericides can be
        applied early to slow the spread in surrounding healthy plants.'
    - diseaseName: Brown Streak Disease (CBSD)
      solution: 'Prevention: Use virus-resistant hybrid varieties. Implement strict
        quarantine measures to prevent moving infected plant material.


        Action:
        This is a viral disease primarily spread by the whitefly. You must uproot
        and destroy symptomatic plants (rogueing) completely, including the roots.


        Treatment:
        Apply systemic insecticides (e.g., Imidacloprid) to control the whitefly
        population and prevent them from spreading the virus to neighboring healthy
        crops.'
    - diseaseName: Green Mottle (CGM)
      solution: 'Prevention: Ensure consistent watering and fertilization. Stressed
        plants are highly susceptible. Control broadleaf weeds around the field,
        as they can host the virus.


        Action: The virus can be transmitted
        mechanically. Always clean and disinfect pruning or harvesting tools with
        a 10% bleach solution between plants.


        Treatment: Monitor and control
        mite populations, which are suspected vectors. Use horticultural oils or
        specific miticides if an infestation is observed.'
    - diseaseName: Healthy
      solution: 'Status: The plant shows excellent vigor.


        Maintenance:
        Continue the current regimen of optimal watering and N-P-K (Nitrogen, Phosphorus,
        Potassium) fertilization. Ensure the soil remains well-drained to prevent
        root rot. No chemical treatment is required.'
    - diseaseName: Mosaic Disease (CMD)
      solution: 'Prevention: Strictly use CMD-resistant (Cassava Mosaic Disease)
        varieties. Never use cuttings from plants that showed even mild yellowing
        the previous year.


        Action: Incurable viral disease. Destroy the
        affected plant urgently. Do not leave the pulled plants in the field; they
        must be burned or buried deep.


        Treatment: Install yellow sticky
        traps around the field perimeter to monitor and trap whiteflies. Use insecticidal
        soaps or chemical insecticides to manage the vector population.'
    confidenceThreshold: 0.25
  - buttonID: cherry
    modelAsset: {fileID: 5022602860645237092, guid: 657558661eab091418d04384c05aab4b, type: 3}
    diseases:
    - diseaseName: healthy
      solution: 'Status: The tree is healthy.


        Maintenance: Keep the base
        of the trunk clean and weed-free to prevent rodent damage and crown rot.
        Prune to maintain an open-center canopy for maximum light exposure.'
    - diseaseName: powdery mildew
      solution: 'Prevention: Avoid overhead watering directly on the tree''s foliage,
        as high humidity without direct rainfall favors this specific fungus.


        Action:
        Prune out and destroy severely infected, stunted white shoots during the
        dormant season or as soon as they appear in spring.


        Treatment:
        Apply Sulfur-based sprays, Potassium bicarbonate, or systemic fungicides
        starting at petal fall and repeating every 2 weeks if the weather remains
        warm and humid.'
    confidenceThreshold: 0.25
  - buttonID: grape
    modelAsset: {fileID: 5022602860645237092, guid: 4846284120e6df64fa1c0a3ab2270369, type: 3}
    diseases:
    - diseaseName: black rot
      solution: 'Prevention: Strict vineyard sanitation is mandatory. Remove all
        mummified grapes from the vines and the ground during winter pruning.


        Action:
        Practice shoot thinning and leaf pulling around the grape clusters to allow
        rapid drying of morning dew.


        Treatment: This is the most destructive
        grape disease. Treat with fungicides (e.g., Mancozeb, Myclobutanil) starting
        when shoots are 2-4 inches long and continue every 10-14 days until the berries
        begin to change color (veraison).'
    - diseaseName: esca (black measles)
      solution: 'Prevention: Avoid pruning vines during wet weather, as the fungal
        spores infect open wounds via splashing rain.


        Action: This is a
        fatal wood-decay disease. Perform "trunk renewal surgery" by cutting the
        trunk down until you reach perfectly clean, white, healthy tissue, and retrain
        a new shoot from the base.


        Treatment: Disinfect pruning shears
        with alcohol after every cut. Seal all pruning wounds larger than 1 inch
        with a protective healing mastic or fungicide paint.'
    - diseaseName: healthy
      solution: 'Status: Healthy grapevine with clean foliage and developing clusters.


        Maintenance:
        Do not neglect summer canopy management (suckering, shoot positioning, and
        leaf removal) to maintain airflow. Continue basic preventative sulfur sprays
        for powdery mildew.'
    - diseaseName: leaf blight (isariopsis leaf spot)
      solution: 'Prevention: Rake and burn fallen leaves at the end of the season
        to reduce the overwintering inoculum.


        Action: Improve vineyard
        airflow by managing the canopy and removing excessive vegetative growth.


        Treatment:
        Apply Copper-based fungicides or broad-spectrum protectants early in the
        season. Usually, spray programs designed for Downy Mildew will also control
        Leaf Blight.'
    confidenceThreshold: 0.25
  - buttonID: orange
    modelAsset: {fileID: 5022602860645237092, guid: 503403ebc74912d4d8f7f54a84ade893, type: 3}
    diseases:
    - diseaseName: haunglongbing (citrus greening)
      solution: 'Prevention: Never transport citrus plants, budwood, or fruit across
        regional quarantine lines. Buy trees only from certified nurseries.


        Action:
        This is a devastating, incurable bacterial disease. The tree will eventually
        die and infect others. It must be cut down, uprooted, and burned immediately.


        Treatment:
        Apply strong, systemic insecticides (like Imidacloprid) in the orchard to
        eliminate the Asian Citrus Psyllid, the insect responsible for injecting
        the bacteria into the trees. Nutritional foliar sprays can temporarily mask
        symptoms but will not cure the tree.'
    confidenceThreshold: 0.25
  - buttonID: peach
    modelAsset: {fileID: 5022602860645237092, guid: 9adda480b0f61164d9f1b15a2d5cb68a, type: 3}
    diseases:
    - diseaseName: bacterial spot
      solution: 'Prevention: Plant resistant peach varieties. Avoid planting peaches
        near susceptible plum varieties which can act as a reservoir for the bacteria.


        Action:
        Keep trees well-fertilized and irrigated; nutritionally stressed plants or
        those growing in sandy soils are highly vulnerable to bacterial spot.


        Treatment:
        Apply Copper-based sprays (like Bordeaux mixture) twice in the autumn: once
        at 20% leaf drop and again at 80% leaf drop. Follow up with a lighter copper
        or Oxytetracycline spray in early spring.'
    - diseaseName: healthy
      solution: 'Status: Vigorous, healthy peach tree.


        Maintenance: Follow
        the standard spraying schedule. It is critical to apply a dormant Copper
        spray in late winter to prevent Peach Leaf Curl, even if the tree looks perfectly
        healthy now. Thin young fruits to prevent branches from breaking under excess
        weight.'
    confidenceThreshold: 0.25
  - buttonID: pepper
    modelAsset: {fileID: 5022602860645237092, guid: 4e1fdf710dc9f3346839f653d20bf48a, type: 3}
    diseases:
    - diseaseName: bacterial spot
      solution: "Prevention: Buy certified pathogen-free seeds, or treat seeds with
        a hot water bath (50\xB0C for 25 mins) before planting.\n\nAction: Remove
        and destroy heavily compromised plants. Avoid overhead sprinkler irrigation
        at all costs; water splashing spreads the bacteria. Use drip irrigation exclusively.\n\nTreatment:
        Chemical control is difficult once established. Spray the remaining healthy
        plants preventively with a tank mix of Copper and Mancozeb to create a protective
        barrier."
    - diseaseName: healthy
      solution: 'Status: Clean and healthy pepper plant.


        Maintenance: Maintain
        consistent, even soil moisture. Fluctuations between extremely dry and extremely
        wet soil will cause Calcium deficiencies, leading to Blossom End Rot on the
        bottom of the peppers. Consider a Calcium Nitrate side-dress.'
    confidenceThreshold: 0.25
  - buttonID: potato
    modelAsset: {fileID: 5022602860645237092, guid: 97ac9c54afd75b04094eae86af08a7e9, type: 3}
    diseases:
    - diseaseName: early blight
      solution: 'Prevention: Ensure an optimal supply of nitrogen and water. Early
        blight primarily attacks older, stressed, or nutrient-deficient leaves.


        Action:
        Practice a 2-to-3-year crop rotation away from potatoes, tomatoes, and eggplants
        (the nightshade family).


        Treatment: Apply a contact fungicide (like
        Chlorothalonil or Mancozeb) at the first signs of target-like, concentric
        ring spots on the lower leaves.'
    - diseaseName: healthy
      solution: 'Status: Healthy, vigorous potato crop.


        Maintenance: Perform
        "hilling" (mounding soil around the base of the plant) to protect growing
        tubers from sunlight (which turns them green and toxic). Monitor closely
        for the Colorado potato beetle and spray if thresholds are met.'
    - diseaseName: late blight
      solution: 'Prevention: Destroy all "cull piles" (discarded, rotting potatoes
        from last year) before planting, as they are the primary source of the blight
        fungus.


        Action: This is an agricultural emergency. Do not allow
        water to pool in the field. Urgently pull, bag, and destroy the focal points
        (completely browned, rotting plants).


        Treatment: Immediately apply
        specific systemic fungicides (like Mefenoxam, Chlorothalonil, or Ridomil
        Gold) to the entire field prior to the next rainfall.'
    confidenceThreshold: 0.25
  - buttonID: rice
    modelAsset: {fileID: 5022602860645237092, guid: 1a41834b193996f41bd91f173e7e3da9, type: 3}
    diseases:
    - diseaseName: BrownSpot
      solution: 'Prevention: This disease is a strong indicator of poor, nutrient-deficient
        soil. Perform a soil test and adjust nutrients, specifically adding Nitrogen
        and Potassium.


        Action: Ensure optimal water levels in the field;
        avoid prolonged periods of dry soil during the growing season.


        Treatment:
        Treat seeds before planting. For active infections, apply a foliar fungicide
        containing Mancozeb or Propiconazole at the first sign of leaf spotting.'
    - diseaseName: Healthy
      solution: 'Status: The crop is healthy and developing normally.


        Maintenance:
        Maintain adequate water levels using AWD (Alternate Wetting and Drying) techniques
        if applicable, to conserve water while preventing fungal overgrowth. Apply
        fertilizers according to the growth stage.'
    - diseaseName: Hispa
      solution: 'Prevention: Keep bunds (levees) and the surrounding field clean
        of grassy weeds, which harbor the adult insects during the off-season.


        Action:
        Clip off the top 5 cm of the rice leaves before transplanting to remove insect
        eggs. For small fields, use sweep nets to manually remove adult beetles.


        Treatment:
        In case of a severe economic threshold infestation, apply a systemic insecticide
        such as Chlorpyrifos or Cypermethrin.'
    - diseaseName: LeafBlast
      solution: 'Prevention: Avoid sudden, heavy applications of Nitrogen fertilizer,
        as overly lush growth is highly susceptible to the blast fungus.


        Action:
        Do not let the field dry out completely, especially during the tillering
        and heading stages. Maintain continuous flooding if possible.


        Treatment:
        Apply specific systemic fungicides (e.g., Tricyclazole or Isoprothiolane)
        as soon as the typical diamond-shaped lesions appear on the leaves.'
    confidenceThreshold: 0.25
  - buttonID: squash
    modelAsset: {fileID: 5022602860645237092, guid: e2e9d79fce585d545854656a2ba9321e, type: 3}
    diseases:
    - diseaseName: powdery mildew
      solution: "Prevention: Plant in full sunlight. Prune overly dense lower leaves
        to allow sunlight to penetrate the canopy and dry the soil.\n\nAction: Avoid
        getting leaves wet in the late afternoon or evening. Water strictly at the
        base of the plant in the morning.\n\nTreatment: Apply preventative treatments
        of Neem oil, Horticultural oils, or Potassium bicarbonate when you notice
        the first white, talcum-powder-like spots. Sulfur is highly effective but
        do not apply it if temperatures exceed 32\xB0C (90\xB0F) or it will burn
        the plant."
    confidenceThreshold: 0.25
  - buttonID: strawberry
    modelAsset: {fileID: 5022602860645237092, guid: c17f8a36e16bb2744b371da88b9bb94d, type: 3}
    diseases:
    - diseaseName: healthy
      solution: 'Status: Healthy, productive strawberry plants.


        Maintenance:
        Keep a fresh, thick layer of straw mulch under the fruits to prevent them
        from resting on wet soil, which causes fruit rot. "Renovate" the beds immediately
        after the final harvest by mowing old foliage and fertilizing.'
    - diseaseName: leaf scorch
      solution: 'Prevention: Thin the plant rows if runners have multiplied too densely.
        Good air circulation is the best defense against fungal leaf spots.


        Action:
        Remove and burn old, dead, or heavily spotted leaves during the post-harvest
        renovation process to prevent infecting new autumn growth.


        Treatment:
        Apply a broad-spectrum protective fungicide (like Captan or Myclobutanil)
        during early spring growth and immediately after bed renovation.'
    confidenceThreshold: 0.25
  - buttonID: tomato
    modelAsset: {fileID: 5022602860645237092, guid: 5276a58ab889b3e4b942fe02e058a342, type: 3}
    diseases:
    - diseaseName: bacterial spot
      solution: 'Prevention: Use disease-free seeds or treat seeds with hot water.
        Practice a strict 3-year crop rotation.


        Action: There is no cure.
        Uproot and bag the heavily infected plants to remove the bacterial source.
        Disinfect your hands and tools with bleach after touching sick plants to
        avoid spreading it down the row.


        Treatment: Spray the remaining
        healthy crop with a Copper-based bactericide every 7-10 days, especially
        after heavy rains, to slow the spread.'
    - diseaseName: early blight
      solution: 'Prevention: Cover the soil with plastic mulch or thick straw. This
        creates a barrier, stopping soil-dwelling fungal spores from splashing onto
        the bottom leaves during heavy rain.


        Action: Prune (sucker) all
        foliage and branches from the bottom 12 inches of the main stem so no leaves
        touch the dirt.


        Treatment: Apply protective fungicides like Chlorothalonil
        or Copper starting when the first fruits are the size of a walnut.'
    - diseaseName: healthy
      solution: 'Status: Excellent, disease-free tomato plant.


        Maintenance:
        Ventilate the greenhouse heavily. Remove side suckers on time to direct energy
        to the fruit. Use foliar Calcium sprays preventively to avoid Blossom End
        Rot.'
    - diseaseName: late blight
      solution: 'Prevention: Water plants only at the base via drip tape. Never use
        overhead sprinklers.


        Action: If you see large oily spots and white
        fuzz on the underside of leaves, act immediately. Destroy the infected plants
        (do not compost them). Massively ventilate the greenhouse to drop humidity.


        Treatment:
        Apply a strong systemic fungicide (Mancozeb or Copper for organic). This
        disease caused the Irish Potato Famine and can destroy a greenhouse in under
        3 days.'
    - diseaseName: leaf mold
      solution: 'Prevention: This fungus thrives exclusively in stagnant, highly
        humid air (above 85% RH). Urgently lower the humidity by opening all vents
        and doors of the greenhouse.


        Action: Cut off the lowest, most affected
        leaves that show yellow spots on top and olive-green mold on the bottom.
        Space plants further apart next season.


        Treatment: Apply Copper
        or Chlorothalonil-based fungicides, ensuring you spray the undersides of
        the leaves where the mold grows.'
    - diseaseName: septoria leaf spot
      solution: 'Prevention: Eliminate all weeds from the nightshade family (like
        horsenettle or Jimsonweed) near the garden, as they host the disease.


        Action:
        Remove lower leaves showing small circular spots with a gray center and dark
        margins. Do not water the plants with a hose from above.


        Treatment:
        Treat with protective fungicides (Chlorothalonil or Copper) starting as soon
        as symptoms appear, repeating every 7-10 days.'
    - diseaseName: spider mites two-spotted spider mite
      solution: 'Prevention: Mites proliferate explosively in hot, dry, dusty environments.
        Slightly increasing the air humidity and spraying the greenhouse pathways
        with water to keep dust down can slow them.


        Action: Isolate the
        plant and cut off dry, stippled, parchment-like leaves with visible webbing.


        Treatment:
        Standard insecticides do not kill mites. You must apply a specific Miticide
        (Abamectin, Spiromesifen) or coat the leaves thoroughly with Neem oil. For
        organic control, release predatory mites (Phytoseiulus persimilis).'
    - diseaseName: target spot
      solution: 'Prevention: Ensure the greenhouse has adequate exhaust fans. Thin
        out the dense inner foliage of the plant to allow air and light to dry the
        interior stems.


        Action: Avoid working among the tomatoes early
        in the morning when they are wet with dew, as your clothes will spread the
        fungal spores.


        Treatment: Apply contact fungicide treatments (like
        Chlorothalonil or Mancozeb) focused on the middle and lower canopy.'
    - diseaseName: tomato mosaic virus
      solution: 'Prevention: Wash your hands thoroughly with soap and water before
        handling plants. Do not smoke or handle commercial tobacco near the greenhouse,
        as the virus can originate from tobacco products.


        Action: The disease
        is incurable and highly contagious through simple physical contact. Uproot
        and burn the plant immediately.


        Treatment: Disinfect all pruning
        tools with a 20% dry milk solution or a 10% bleach solution to denature the
        virus proteins.'
    - diseaseName: tomato yellow leaf curl virus
      solution: 'Prevention: Install fine-mesh exclusion screens on greenhouse vents
        to physically block the Sweetpotato Whitefly from entering.


        Action:
        Remove bushes with yellowed, upward-curled leaves and severely stunted (bonsai-like)
        growth. Place the plant in a plastic bag before carrying it out of the greenhouse
        so whiteflies don''t scatter.


        Treatment: Install yellow sticky
        traps. Apply systemic insecticides or insecticidal soaps to target the whitefly
        vector. The virus itself cannot be cured.'
    confidenceThreshold: 0.25
  ModelNameTextMeshProUGUI: {fileID: 304749073}
  DiseaseNameTextMeshProUGUI: {fileID: 693482344}
  SolutionDiseaseTextMeshProUGUI: {fileID: 466523441}
